# frozen_string_literal: true

# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now.
#
# **A write to something outside this process** — a payment taken, an invoice sent, a
# webhook delivered. The `Io` prefix on the class name means the crossing is visible at
# every call site without opening anything.
#
# It is a **sister of Command**, not a subclass of one: every class inherits exactly one
# base and no deeper, so the contract is repeated here rather than inherited.
#
# **Why it is a separate kind at all, and the reason is the transaction.** A command is
# exactly one transaction. An external call made inside one holds a database transaction
# open across a network round trip — the classic way a slow third party takes a database
# down — and the remote write cannot be rolled back by it in any case. So a command may not
# do IO. The external call and the local write that records its result are two steps,
# sequenced by a workflow, which is the only kind that has accepted the bill for spanning
# them: each step idempotent, each intermediate state legal.
#
# **It opens no transaction and touches no record**, for the same reason. What it changes is
# somebody else's, and what it answers with is a Result the caller can act on.
#
# **Assume it will be retried.** A workflow's only recovery is to run the sequence again, so
# send an idempotency key where the far end offers one, and where it does not, say so in the
# subclass rather than hoping.
class IoCommand
  include TypedArguments

<%- if auth -%>
  extend Permission
<%- end -%>

  # The check runs here, once, for every command — not in each `call`, where it would be a
  # line every operation has to remember and one operation would forget. It opens no transaction of its own.
  #
  # An operation implementing `anonymous_call` instead of `call` runs before anyone is
  # identified and is not checked. That is a property of the class, never of the caller.
<%- if auth -%>
  def self.call(actor: nil, **arguments)
    return Result.failure(:forbidden) unless permits?(actor)

    operation = new(**arguments)
    result = operation.__perform__(actor)
<%- else -%>
  def self.call(**arguments)
    result = new(**arguments).__perform__
<%- end -%>

    raise TypeError, "#{name}#call must answer with a Result, got #{result.class}" unless result.is_a?(Result)

    result
  end

  private
<%- if auth -%>

  # Whoever the door was opened for. Most operations never read it, which is why it is here
  # rather than in every initializer.
  attr_reader :actor

<%- end -%>

  def success(value = nil)
    Result.success(value)
  end

  def failure(error)
    Result.failure(error)
  end

  # **Dispatches with an implicit receiver**, so it works whether the operation left its
  # entry point public or made it private. `send` would work too and is banned: it steps
  # over `private`, routes around the call graph, and names a method nobody can grep for. A
  # base class that writes it cannot ask an application not to.
  #
  # Public, and unreachable all the same: `new` is private, so nobody outside can hold an
  # operation to call this on — and `Shipshape/OnlyTheDoorIsCalled` refuses the attempt at
  # the call site, where no visibility keyword can be stepped over.
<%- if auth -%>
  public def __perform__(actor)
    # **The actor belongs to the base class, not to the operation's signature.** The check
    # above needed it; most operations do not, and forcing `actor:` into every initializer
    # would put a keyword in fifty constructors so that three could read it. Set here, and
    # available to any operation that wants it through the private reader below.
    @actor = actor

    self.class.anonymous? ? anonymous_call : call
  end
<%- else -%>
  public def __perform__
    call
  end
<%- end -%>

  # **The only way in is `call`.** A caller cannot build an operation, so it cannot reach one
  # that skipped the permission check, the transaction or the return-type assertion.
  #
  # `allocate` is on the list because it is the hole `new` alone leaves: it is public on every
  # Ruby class, it skips `initialize` entirely, and `SettleInvoice.allocate.__perform__(actor)`
  # ran the operation unauthenticated. Verified by running it, not by reading it.
  private_class_method :new, :allocate
end
